1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:11,310
Paying a team in Zcash usually means a transaction per person. Devoid pays the whole list at once, privately. Here's a payout, start to finish.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,528
It's a Zcash wallet built for one job: paying many people in a single shielded transaction.

3
00:00:19,233 --> 00:00:28,497
You bring a list, check it, and sign once. Up to twenty-five people go in each shielded transaction, and longer lists go out in order.

4
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:40,432
This is a demo wallet on a simulated chain. Play money, but the real app, running entirely in your browser. No account, and your keys never leave your device.

5
00:00:41,133 --> 00:00:51,333
Let's pay three contributors. I'll paste the list into "Send" and hit "Check recipients". Every address is validated, so a typo can't cost you.

6
00:00:52,033 --> 00:00:58,081
Devoid totals it up as you go: what everyone gets, the network fee, and what you'll have left.

7
00:00:59,167 --> 00:01:07,015
Then one review screen, with everything in one place: three recipients, the total, and the exact fee, before you sign.

8
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:17,300
One passphrase, one confirmation, and it's sent. Here it's simulated; on a real wallet, this same screen builds, proves and broadcasts it.

9
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,648
In "Activity", it reads "Sent to 3 recipients": three people paid, in a single transaction.

10
00:01:25,333 --> 00:01:35,293
And it's not a mock-up. Devoid's Rust engine has sent shielded payments that were mined on the Zcash mainnet, from the desktop app and from the browser.

11
00:01:36,750 --> 00:01:42,174
One transaction. Multiple payments. Devoid is open source, on GitHub.
